## Proof

Let $R = \mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ be the local ring of the complex manifold $X$ at the point $x$, with maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m}_x$ consisting of germs vanishing at $x$. The residue field is
$$\mathbb{C}(x) = R/\mathfrak{m}_x \cong \mathbb{C},$$
viewed as an $R$-algebra via the canonical quotient map $\pi: R \to R/\mathfrak{m}_x$.

We compute $\mathbb{C}(x) \otimes_R \mathbb{C}(x)$.

**Key isomorphism.** For any commutative ring $R$, any ideal $I \subseteq R$, and any $R$-module $M$, there is a natural isomorphism of $R$-modules
$$(R/I) \otimes_R M \;\cong\; M/IM,$$
given explicitly by $\overline{a} \otimes m \mapsto \overline{am}$, with inverse $\overline{m} \mapsto \overline{1}\otimes m$. (This follows from the right-exactness of the tensor product applied to the exact sequence $0 \to I \to R \to R/I \to 0$.)

**Applying it.** Set $I = \mathfrak{m}_x$ and $M = \mathbb{C}(x) = R/\mathfrak{m}_x$. Then
$$\mathbb{C}(x) \otimes_R \mathbb{C}(x) \;=\; (R/\mathfrak{m}_x)\otimes_R (R/\mathfrak{m}_x) \;\cong\; (R/\mathfrak{m}_x)\,/\,\mathfrak{m}_x\cdot(R/\mathfrak{m}_x).$$

**The ideal action vanishes.** Since $\mathfrak{m}_x$ is the maximal ideal and $R/\mathfrak{m}_x$ is the residue field, every element of $\mathfrak{m}_x$ acts as zero on $R/\mathfrak{m}_x$:
$$\mathfrak{m}_x \cdot (R/\mathfrak{m}_x) = 0.$$

Therefore
$$\mathbb{C}(x) \otimes_R \mathbb{C}(x) \;\cong\; (R/\mathfrak{m}_x)/0 \;=\; R/\mathfrak{m}_x \;=\; \mathbb{C}(x).$$

**The isomorphism is the multiplication map.** Concretely, the isomorphism is
$$\mu: \mathbb{C}(x)\otimes_R \mathbb{C}(x) \xrightarrow{\;\sim\;} \mathbb{C}(x), \qquad \overline{f}\otimes \overline{g} \;\mapsto\; \overline{f}\cdot\overline{g} = \overline{fg}.$$
This is well-defined because the $R$-module structure on both copies of $\mathbb{C}(x)$ is the quotient action. It is surjective (since $\overline{1}\otimes\overline{c}\mapsto\overline{c}$), and the computation above shows the source has the same dimension as the target (both are $1$-dimensional over $\mathbb{C}$), so it is an isomorphism.

**Conclusion.** Yes, the tensor product is still $\mathbb{C}(x)$:
$$\boxed{\mathbb{C}(x)\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_{X,x}}\mathbb{C}(x) \;\cong\; \mathbb{C}(x).}$$

Intuitively, the local ring $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ acts on the residue field $\mathbb{C}(x)$ through its quotient $R/\mathfrak{m}_x$; the maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m}_x$ is killed in both factors, so tensoring over $R$ reduces to tensoring over the residue field itself, where $\mathbb{C}(x)\otimes_{\mathbb{C}(x)}\mathbb{C}(x)\cong\mathbb{C}(x)$ trivially.

